# Prism — the extraction adapter that reads Ruby with Ruby's own parser.
#
# The contract : it DECLARES the port it implements (the inverted arrow) ; the
# port never names it. `extracted_by("Prism")` resolves if and only if the
# extraction port carries the `extracted_by` verb.
#
# Named for what it IS rather than what it does. There is no proxy object
# pretending to be a list, no hand-rolled reader, no re-spelling of the
# predicate : the block records where it was written, Prism turns that file into
# a tree, and the node at that position is the expression. `toppings.size < 10`
# is a genuine Ruby method call at runtime AND a comparison node at extraction
# time — both readings of one text, which is the whole reason the arrow can run
# Ruby -> IR without the IR ever running back.
#
# It declares NO fields. Where a block's source lives is a property of the
# block, so there is nothing a .world could supply.
Hecks.adapter "Prism" do
  port "extraction"
end
